_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "Turnover for the group is flat and low (0.17-0.46) through 9140-20000, then spikes sharply to 1.0 at t\u224823370 and decays back, indicating a single abrupt transition rather than steady drift. Comparing the 5000-unit half-windows around that point (step_03) gives Jaccard = 0.0: all 126 pre-change group edges disappear and 106 entirely new edges appear, with the old contact set {65,74,163,292,389,442,880,978,987,1200,1587,1633,1780,1888,1999} replaced wholesale by {66,158,232,245,410,422,526,702,772,848,850,986,990,1317,1358,1456,1566,1681}. A control diff in the earlier, quiescent part of the period (step_04) shows ordinary background turnover (Jaccard 0.49), so full replacement is not the group's normal behaviour. Fine-grained checks confirm abruptness: node 25 still contacts old partners 442/1587 up to 23370, has a ~2000-unit silent gap, and thereafter only contacts the new set (steps 05-07, 10). Confounders are controlled: group event volume is comparable before and after the switch (~95-107 per bin, step_02), so this is not a volume or growth artifact; and the old partners 442/1587 are highly active before but nearly dead after, while new partners 986/66 are nearly absent before and highly active after (steps 11-12), so it is not driven by global popularity trends. Ignoring time, node 25 is never connected to 442 after or to 986 before (steps 08-09), confirming the partner sets are temporally disjoint."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_798d04c6\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [25, 396, 489, 638, 1036, 1163, 1244, 1366, 1417, 1489, 1729, 1763],\n  \"window\": [18370, 28371],\n  \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_